<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#latin">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Latin is the language of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which spread across the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> with Roman rule and became the root of the Romance languages. It remained the language of the Church, learning and administration in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. Through Spanish and Portuguese, its descendants became the dominant languages of the Spanish and Portuguese empires and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</p></div>
